1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,500
One sky. The Tupinambá saw — an ostrich
hungry for eggs.

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:14,500
1612: a French fort on Maranhão Island,
Brazil. Four Capuchin friars came with
the ships.

3
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:19,500
One, Claude d’Abbeville, published a
book in Paris in 1614.

4
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:25,500
Nearly everyone knew most of their
stars, each by a name the ancestors
gave.

5
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:33,000
Then he copied 28 names: a rainy jaw, a
nine-star grill, a monkey, a boy eating
soup.

6
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:40,500
Then: “and many others, which I leave
out.” Mostly, he never said which stars.

7
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:45,000
The island’s Tupinambá later vanished
through war, disease and slavery.

8
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,500
Nearly 400 years later, astronomer
Germano Afonso watched the sky with the
Guarani.

9
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:55,500
Distant relatives, 2,500 km apart, with
similar skies. Some names came back.

10
00:00:55,500 --> 00:01:02,500
The grandest, Yandoutin: Afonso traced
it as a rhea, from the Southern Cross to
Scorpius.

11
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:07,000
Its beak reaches for two stars:
Ouyra-Oupia, “the two eggs”.

12
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:14,000
The Guarani say the Southern Cross grips
its head. Let go, and it drinks the
earth dry.

13
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Its body, Scorpius, rises in the
southeast. From mid-northern latitudes,
its head never rises.

14
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Bugis: the sailors’ star compass.

15
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

